OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026", "title": "Hardness of finding large independent sets in three-colorable graphs", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026.pdf", "family": "106", "family_title": "Hardness of coloring three-colorable graphs", "abstract_html": "We prove that, for every fixed \\(0\\lt \\delta\\lt 1/3\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish three-colorable graphs from graphs in which every independent set has fewer than <i>δ</i> times the number of vertices. Consequently, for every fixed integer <i>c</i> ≥ 3, finding a proper <i>c</i>-coloring of a three-colorable graph is NP-hard.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm \\(R_\\delta\\) mapping every explicitly encoded Boolean \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\varphi\\) to a nonempty finite simple undirected unweighted graph \\(G_\\varphi\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Theorem \\(1.1\\) of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">A proper coloring assigns different colors to adjacent vertices. An independent set consists of pairwise nonadjacent vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonempty finite graph \\(G\\), let \\(\\alpha(G)\\) be its maximum independent-set size.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a real number \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm \\(R_\\delta\\) mapping every explicitly encoded Boolean \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\varphi\\) to a nonempty finite simple undirected unweighted graph \\(G_\\varphi\\) such that\n\\[\n\\varphi\\text{ is satisfiable}\\Longrightarrow G_\\varphi\\text{ is three-colorable},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\varphi\\text{ is unsatisfiable}\\Longrightarrow\n\\alpha(G_\\varphi)&lt;\\delta |V(G_\\varphi)|.\n\\]\nThe running time and explicit output size are polynomial in the ordinary binary encoding length of \\(\\varphi\\). Their constants and polynomial degree may depend on the fixed \\(\\delta\\), but not on \\(\\varphi\\). The coloring in the first implication covers every vertex and is not supplied with the graph.</p>", "proof": "<p>It suffices to prove the theorem for rational \\(\\delta\\). For a fixed real threshold, choose a fixed rational \\(0&lt;\\delta_0&lt;\\delta\\) and use the reduction for \\(\\delta_0\\).</p><p>Fix rational \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/3\\), and choose a rational\n\\[\n0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\delta/8.\n\\]\nSet \\(L=64\\), and take \\(\\beta,K\\) from Proposition \\(2\\) for \\(L,\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Choose a positive integer \\(m\\) with\n\\[\nL/m&lt;\\beta/2,\n\\]\nthen a rational \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\) with\n\\[\n\\lambda K&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThe coefficient law (1) is now fixed. Since its probability \\(p_m\\) of a full coefficient is positive, choose an integer \\(s\\ge2\\) with\n\\[\n(1-p_m)^s&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p><p>Next choose \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) sufficiently small that\n\\[\n\\frac{s(2\\gamma)^2}{\\beta^2}&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\]\nand then choose an even positive integer \\(P\\) sufficiently large that, with \\(D=mP\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{2L}{D}&lt;\\gamma,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{2Ls}{P}&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThis establishes every inequality in (14).</p><p>Lemma \\(5.2\\) gives the alphabet-independent bound\n\\[\nd=\\max\\{1,J(sL,\\gamma)(m+1)^s\\}.\n\\]\nApply Proposition \\(6\\) to \\(s,d,\\eta=\\varepsilon\\), obtaining \\(r\\ge s\\) and a rational law \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\binom{[r]}s\\). Finally choose a rational \\(\\sigma\\in(0,1)\\) with\n\\[\n4^r d^2\\sigma&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{31}\n\\]\nOnly now invoke Proposition \\(1\\) for this fixed \\(\\sigma\\). Its constant alphabets may be large, but no preceding choice depends on their sizes or on the input formula.</p><p>All objects used to run the reduction are finite integers or finite rational probability tables. For each fixed \\(\\delta\\), choose them once and incorporate their finite descriptions into one algorithm. Neither a uniform runtime as \\(\\delta\\to0\\) nor an algorithm computing all constants from \\(\\delta\\) is asserted or needed. There is no input-dependent advice. The real tolerances, continuous functions, junta approximants, and alignments enter only the analysis.</p><p>Choose a fixed integer \\(q&gt;1/\\delta\\). Before invoking Label Cover, reindex the occurring variables densely, preserving repeated occurrences of each variable. Remove tautological clauses and duplicate literals.</p><p>A formula with no clauses maps directly to a one-vertex graph. A formula containing an empty clause maps directly to \\(K_q\\). Pad a remaining short clause to width three by including all sign choices for fresh filler variables. This preserves satisfiability at constant overhead.</p><p>For every remaining input, apply the Label Cover reduction of Proposition \\(1\\) and the graph construction of Proposition \\(3\\). Proposition \\(3\\) gives deterministic polynomial time and explicit output size, in the ordinary binary input length, and a nonempty finite simple undirected unweighted graph.</p><p>The two preprocessing branches have the required promises. A formula with no remaining clauses is satisfiable and produces a one-vertex graph. An empty clause certifies unsatisfiability and produces \\(K_q\\), whose independence ratio is\n\\[\n1/q&lt;\\delta.\n\\]</p><p>If the formula is satisfiable, its Label Cover instance has value one, and Proposition \\(4\\) supplies a proper three-coloring of every output vertex.</p><p>Suppose instead that the formula is unsatisfiable. Its Label Cover value is at most \\(\\sigma\\). If the metric clique branch occurs, the output again has independent-set density \\(1/q&lt;\\delta\\).</p><p>Otherwise, let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be any nonempty independent set of the output graph. Proposition \\(5\\) supplies the associated functions and terminal lists. Proposition \\(7\\) and (31) bound the probability of an unaligned chain–\\(B\\) pair by\n\\[\n4^r d^2\\sigma+\\varepsilon&lt;2\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nAt every aligned pair, Proposition \\(8\\) bounds the conditional probability of sampling \\(\\mathcal A\\) by \\(6\\varepsilon\\); at other pairs it is at most one. The multiplicity expansion of Proposition \\(3\\) makes this sampling law exactly the uniform law on output vertices. Hence\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\mathcal A|}{|V(G)|}\n\\le\n6\\varepsilon+2\\varepsilon\n=\n8\\varepsilon\n&lt;\n\\delta.\n\\]\nThe empty independent set satisfies the same strict bound because the graph is nonempty. Maximizing over independent sets proves the soundness promise.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "It is NP-hard to distinguish \\(k\\)-colorable graphs from graphs that are not \\(c\\)-colorable.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Corollary \\(1.2\\) of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix integers \\(3\\le k\\le c\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>It is NP-hard to distinguish \\(k\\)-colorable graphs from graphs that are not \\(c\\)-colorable.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a rational\n\\[\n0&lt;\\delta&lt;\\min\\{1/3,1/c\\}\n\\]\nand apply Theorem \\(1\\). A three-colorable graph is \\(k\\)-colorable. A \\(c\\)-colorable graph has an independent set of size at least \\(n/c\\), which the soundness bound excludes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are nonempty constant alphabets and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to explicit Label Cover instances with nonempty occurrence lists such that satisfiable formulas have value \\(1\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is the standard input stated as Theorem \\(2.1\\), “Perfect-completeness Label Cover,” in the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">A Label Cover instance consists of finite question sets \\(U,V\\), nonempty finite answer alphabets \\(\\Sigma_L,\\Sigma_R\\), and a nonempty explicit list \\(\\mathcal C\\) of test occurrences.</p><p class=\"li\">Each occurrence \\(c\\in\\mathcal C\\) has questions \\(u_c\\in U\\), \\(v_c\\in V\\), and a total map\n\\[\n\\pi_c:\\Sigma_L\\longrightarrow\\Sigma_R.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Occurrences, including repeated occurrences, are sampled uniformly, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(\\mathcal C)\n=\n\\max_{\\ell:U\\to\\Sigma_L,\\ \\rho:V\\to\\Sigma_R}\n\\Pr_{c\\in\\mathcal C}\n\\bigl[\\pi_c(\\ell(u_c))=\\rho(v_c)\\bigr].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a rational \\(\\sigma\\in(0,1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are nonempty constant alphabets and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to explicit Label Cover instances with nonempty occurrence lists such that satisfiable formulas have value \\(1\\) and unsatisfiable formulas have value at most \\(\\sigma\\). Unused questions may be removed.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the standard consequence of the PCP Theorem and parallel repetition [2, 24]. A quantitative statement is [11, Theorem \\(6.2\\), full version]: for a fixed number \\(k\\) of repetitions, the output size is \\(n^{O(k)}\\), the alphabet size is at most \\(a^k\\), and the soundness is at most \\(\\beta_0^k\\), for absolute constants \\(a&gt;1\\) and \\(0&lt;\\beta_0&lt;1\\). Choose a fixed \\(k\\) for which \\(\\beta_0^k\\le \\sigma\\).</p><p>The unweighted total-projection formulation is explicitly recorded in [8, Definitions \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.2\\) and Theorem \\(1.3\\)]. That source even supplies \\(d\\)-to-one maps, a property not used here. The verifier’s random choices specify an explicit list of constraints; they are not random choices of the reduction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are \\(\\beta>0\\) and an integer \\(K\\ge1\\), independent of \\(s\\), such that every \\(L\\)-Lipschitz function \\(H:\\mathbb T^s\\to[-1,1]\\) satisfies:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Lemma \\(2.3\\), “Two dimension-independent bounds,” of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">The circle \\(\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) has distance\n\\[\nd_{\\mathbb T}(x,y)=\\min_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|x-y-k|.\n\\]\nAll products of metric spaces carry the sup metric.</p><p class=\"li\">Uniform inputs on cubes have product Lebesgue law; uniform inputs on products of circles have product Haar law. All norms use the indicated probability measure.</p><p class=\"li\">For a square-integrable function \\(H\\) of independent circle coordinates \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_s\\), let \\(E_i\\) average coordinate \\(z_i\\) alone, and define\n\\[\nQ_i=\\operatorname{id}-E_i,\n\\qquad\nD_i(H)=\\|Q_iH\\|_2.\n\\]\nBoth \\(E_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) are orthogonal projections.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0\\le L&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\beta&gt;0\\) and an integer \\(K\\ge1\\), independent of \\(s\\), such that every \\(L\\)-Lipschitz function \\(H:\\mathbb T^s\\to[-1,1]\\) satisfies:\n\\[\n\\mathbb EH=0,\\quad \\mathbb EH^2&gt;\\varepsilon\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nD_i(H)\\ge\\beta\\text{ for some }i;\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\#\\{i:D_i(H)\\ge\\beta/2\\}\\le K.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nJ=J(L,\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}/2)\n\\]\nfrom Lemma \\(2.1\\). Choose a set \\(S\\) of at most \\(J\\) coordinates such that\n\\[\nG=\\mathbb E[H\\mid z_S],\n\\qquad\n\\|H-G\\|_2&lt;\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}/2.\n\\]\nIf \\(H\\) has mean zero and squared norm exceeding \\(\\varepsilon\\), then \\(G\\) has mean zero and, by orthogonality,\n\\[\n\\|G\\|_2^2&gt;3\\varepsilon/4,\n\\]\nin particular \\(\\|G\\|_2^2&gt;\\varepsilon/4\\).</p><p>For completeness, decompose\n\\[\nH=\\sum_{A\\subseteq[s]}H_A,\n\\qquad\nH_A=\n\\left(\\prod_{i\\in A}Q_i\\right)\n\\left(\\prod_{i\\notin A}E_i\\right)H.\n\\]\nThe factors commute and the summands are orthogonal. Thus\n\\[\nG=\\sum_{A\\subseteq S}H_A\n\\]\nand, in the mean-zero case,\n\\[\n\\|G\\|_2^2\n=\n\\sum_{\\varnothing\\ne A\\subseteq S}\\|H_A\\|_2^2\n\\le\n\\sum_{i\\in S}\\sum_{A\\ni i}\\|H_A\\|_2^2\n=\n\\sum_{i\\in S}D_i(H)^2.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\beta=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\varepsilon}{4J}}\n\\]\nworks for the first assertion.</p><p>For the second assertion, use Lemma \\(2.1\\) again with error less than \\(\\beta/2\\), and write \\(G'\\) for the resulting junta, on at most\n\\[\nK=J(L,\\beta/2)\n\\]\ncoordinates. If \\(i\\) is not one of these coordinates, then \\(Q_iG'=0\\), so\n\\[\nD_i(H)\n=\n\\|Q_i(H-G')\\|_2\n\\le\n\\|H-G'\\|_2\n&lt;\n\\beta/2.\n\\]\nThis proves the cardinality bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an integer \\(J(L,\\tau)\\ge1\\) such that every \\(L\\)-Lipschitz function \\( f:[0,1]^N\\longrightarrow[-1,1], \\) for every positive integer \\(N\\), admits a junta on at most \\(J(L,\\tau)\\) coordinates with \\(L^2\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is the standard analytic input stated as Theorem \\(2.2\\), “Dimension-independent junta approximation,” in the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">A function is a junta on \\(S\\) if it depends only on the input coordinates in \\(S\\). Approximating functions need not be continuous.</p><p class=\"li\">Products carry the sup metric and product uniform measure.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0\\le L&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(\\tau&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an integer \\(J(L,\\tau)\\ge1\\) such that every \\(L\\)-Lipschitz function\n\\[\nf:[0,1]^N\\longrightarrow[-1,1],\n\\]\nfor every positive integer \\(N\\), admits a junta on at most \\(J(L,\\tau)\\) coordinates with \\(L^2\\) error less than \\(\\tau\\). The junta may be taken to be a coordinate conditional expectation of \\(f\\).</p><p>The same bound applies to pullbacks of torus functions under \\([0,1]^N\\to\\mathbb T^N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Austin’s continuous junta theorem [3, Theorem \\(1.1\\), arXiv version \\(4\\)] with the usual interval, uniform measure, and modulus of continuity \\(\\omega(t)=Lt\\). The interval is compact, connected, and locally connected, as required there.</p><p>Austin’s theorem gives\n\\[\n\\|f-\\mathbb E[f\\mid X_S]\\|_1&lt;\\tau^2/2\n\\]\nwith \\(|S|\\) bounded independently of \\(N\\). Both functions take values in \\([-1,1]\\), so\n\\[\n\\|f-\\mathbb E[f\\mid X_S]\\|_2^2\n\\le\n2\\|f-\\mathbb E[f\\mid X_S]\\|_1\n&lt;\\tau^2.\n\\]\nThe quotient \\([0,1]^N\\to\\mathbb T^N\\) is nonexpanding and preserves the product uniform law, proving the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For fixed parameters and alphabets, this construction produces a nonempty finite simple undirected unweighted graph in deterministic polynomial time and output size, measured in the size of the explicit Label Cover …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is the graph construction of Section \\(3\\) and Proposition \\(3.1\\), “Explicit deterministic encoding,” of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a rational \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/3\\), an integer \\(q&gt;1/\\delta\\), integers \\(r\\ge s\\ge2\\) and \\(m\\ge1\\), an even positive integer \\(P\\), a rational \\(\\lambda\\in(0,1)\\), and a rational probability distribution \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\binom{[r]}s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nD=mP,\\qquad\n\\Gamma=(D^{-1}\\mathbb Z)/\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_m=\\{0,1/m,\\ldots,1\\},\n\\qquad\np_k=\\Pr(t=k/m)=\\frac{\\lambda^k}{\\sum_{\\ell=0}^m\\lambda^\\ell}\n\\quad(0\\le k\\le m).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a Label Cover instance as in Proposition \\(1\\), with unused questions removed and with fixed alphabets \\(\\Sigma_L,\\Sigma_R\\).</p><p>Construction:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. In layer \\(i\\in[r]\\), take all question tuples\n\\[\n\\mathbf q=(v_1,\\ldots,v_{i-1},u_i,\\ldots,u_{r-1})\n\\in V^{i-1}\\times U^{r-i}.\n\\]\nTheir answer set is\n\\[\nM_i=\\Sigma_R^{i-1}\\times\\Sigma_L^{r-i}.\n\\]\nEach position is a separate coordinate even if question names repeat. Different tuples use separate copies of the answer set.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Between adjacent layers \\(i,i+1\\), impose a map whenever the tuples agree outside position \\(i\\) and their questions at position \\(i\\) are the endpoints of a test occurrence \\(c\\). The answer map applies \\(\\pi_c\\) in position \\(i\\) and the identity elsewhere. Impose it separately for every occurrence.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. At each tuple in layer \\(i\\), place a separate copy of \\(\\Gamma^{M_i}\\), and let \\(\\mathcal X\\) be their disjoint union. Form a finite undirected weighted link graph on \\(\\mathcal X\\):</p><p class=\"li l2\">Within each copy, link any two points with length their sup circle distance.</p><p class=\"li l2\">For every imposed map \\(\\pi:M_i\\to M_{i+1}\\), link each target point \\(x\\in\\Gamma^{M_{i+1}}\\) to its source pullback \\(x\\circ\\pi\\), with length zero.</p><p>Let \\(\\operatorname{dist}\\) be the shortest-path extended pseudometric, with distance infinity between components. Let \\(T\\) add \\(1/2\\) to every phase coordinate within each copy. Since \\(D\\) is even, \\(T\\) permutes \\(\\mathcal X\\), satisfies \\(T^2=\\operatorname{id}\\), and preserves all link lengths.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If some \\(x\\in\\mathcal X\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(x,Tx)\\le1/8,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\noutput \\(K_q\\) and stop.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Otherwise, define output vertices from the following finite probability experiment:</p><p class=\"li l2\">Sample \\(r-1\\) independent uniform occurrences \\(c_1,\\ldots,c_{r-1}\\), with questions \\((u_h,v_h)\\). They determine the chain\n\\[\n\\mathbf q_i=(v_1,\\ldots,v_{i-1},u_i,\\ldots,u_{r-1}),\n\\qquad i\\in[r].\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\pi_{ij}:M_i\\to M_j\\), \\(i\\le j\\), for the composite maps. They apply the test projections in positions \\(i,\\ldots,j-1\\) and are composition-consistent; \\(\\pi_{ii}\\) is the identity.</p><p class=\"li l2\">Independently sample \\(B\\sim\\mu\\). For every \\(j\\in B\\), independently sample \\(t_j\\) from (1) and rotation entries\n\\[\n\\widehat\\theta_{j,\\ell}\n\\text{ uniform in }\\{0,1/P,\\ldots,(P-1)/P\\},\n\\qquad \\ell\\in M_j.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li l2\">With \\(b=\\min B\\), give the outcome the location\n\\[\nx=\n\\left(\n\\sum_{j\\in B}t_j\\widehat\\theta_{j,\\pi_{bj}(\\kappa)}\n\\pmod1\n\\right)_{\\kappa\\in M_b}\n\\in\\Gamma^{M_b}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nin the copy at \\(\\mathbf q_b\\).</p><p>Take distinct output vertices for elementary outcomes, with integer multiplicities making the experiment their exact uniform law. Different vertices may have the same location. For distinct output vertices at locations \\(x,y\\), put an edge precisely when\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(x,Ty)\\le1/8.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For fixed parameters and alphabets, this construction produces a nonempty finite simple undirected unweighted graph in deterministic polynomial time and output size, measured in the size of the explicit Label Cover instance. Composing with Proposition \\(1\\) for fixed \\(\\sigma\\) gives polynomial time and output size in the binary encoding length of the original \\(3\\)-CNF formula, with the preprocessing specified in the proof of Theorem \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The edge rule is symmetric because \\(T\\) is an involutive isometry:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(x,Ty)\n=\n\\operatorname{dist}(Tx,y)\n=\n\\operatorname{dist}(y,Tx).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(N=|\\mathcal C|\\ge1\\). All alphabets, layer counts, and grids are fixed constants. There are polynomially many question tuples, each with a constant-size grid. The imposed maps and links are therefore polynomially enumerable.</p><p>Every finite link length is an integer multiple of \\(1/D\\). After multiplying lengths by \\(D\\), exact integer shortest-path algorithms compute all distances, with infinity stored separately. Shortest paths have polynomial bit length, and comparison with \\(1/8\\) is exact by multiplication by \\(8\\). This also implements the clique test.</p><p>There are exactly \\(N^{r-1}\\) equally likely occurrence chains. For each chain, the remaining outcome probabilities are fixed rational numbers. The number of rotation entries can vary with \\(B\\) and its layers, but it ranges over fixed constants. Choose a common positive denominator \\(C_0\\) for all these conditional probabilities. For a conditional outcome of probability \\(p\\), emit \\(C_0p\\) distinct vertices, omitting zero-probability outcomes. Every chain contributes exactly \\(C_0\\) vertices.</p><p>The total is\n\\[\nC_0N^{r-1},\n\\]\nand uniform sampling of these vertices is exactly the stated experiment, followed by a uniform choice among the copies of the chosen outcome. Exhaustive enumeration uses no random bits. Evaluating (4) for every distinct pair remains polynomial and gives an explicit edge list with no loops or multiple edges.</p><p>The Label Cover reduction is polynomial for fixed \\(\\sigma\\). The initial variable reindexing and clause preprocessing take polynomial time in the ordinary binary input length, and their two trivial branches have constant-size outputs. Thus the construction covers every explicitly encoded \\(3\\)-CNF formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The clique branch does not occur, and the constructed graph has a proper three-coloring covering every output vertex, including every multiplicity copy.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Lemma \\(3.2\\), “All-vertex completeness,” of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the graph construction of Proposition \\(3\\), including its answer sets \\(M_i\\), grid union \\(\\mathcal X\\), shortest-path extended pseudometric \\(\\operatorname{dist}\\), half-shift \\(T\\), clique test (2), and output edge rule (4).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that the Label Cover instance has value \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The clique branch does not occur, and the constructed graph has a proper three-coloring covering every output vertex, including every multiplicity copy.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a labeling satisfying every occurrence. At each question tuple, form the product of its assigned answers, an element of that tuple’s answer set. Evaluate a grid point at this product answer. This defines a map\n\\[\n\\phi:\\mathcal X\\longrightarrow\\mathbb T.\n\\]</p><p>Within a copy, evaluation is nonexpanding for the sup circle distance. Across each zero link the two evaluations agree because the labeling satisfies that test occurrence. Hence \\(\\phi\\) is nonexpanding for every path, and thus for \\(\\operatorname{dist}\\). Also\n\\[\n\\phi(Tx)=\\phi(x)+1/2.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(x,Tx)\\ge1/2\n\\]\nfor every \\(x\\), so the clique test fails.</p><p>If output vertices at \\(x,y\\) are adjacent, then\n\\[\nd_{\\mathbb T}\\bigl(\\phi(x),\\phi(y)+1/2\\bigr)\\le1/8,\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\nd_{\\mathbb T}\\bigl(\\phi(x),\\phi(y)\\bigr)\\ge3/8&gt;1/3.\n\\]\nColor each vertex according to which of the half-open intervals\n\\[\n[0,1/3),\\qquad[1/3,2/3),\\qquad[2/3,1)\n\\]\ncontains its phase. Two phases in the same interval have circle distance less than \\(1/3\\), so this colors every vertex properly. All multiplicity copies receive the color of their location.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every occurrence chain, one can choose lists \\(S_I^j\\subseteq M_j\\), \\(j\\in I\\), with \\( \\sum_{j\\in I}|S_I^j|\\le d, \\) depending only on the subchain’s label sets, internal projection maps, and functions \\(F_j\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This proposition collects the construction of Section \\(4.1\\) and the conclusion of Lemma \\(4.2\\), “Separated lists,” of the paper. Its supporting Lemma \\(5.2\\) below is the paper’s Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\in(0,1)\\), and let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a Label Cover instance with \\(\\operatorname{val}(\\mathcal C)\\le\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the construction of Proposition \\(3\\), with parameters \\(r\\ge s\\ge2\\), \\(m\\ge1\\), even \\(P\\), \\(D=mP\\), and assume that the clique branch does not occur.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be a nonempty independent set in the output graph. Let \\(A\\subseteq\\mathcal X\\) be its set of locations, with multiplicities removed. Set \\(L=64\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define, with \\((u)_+=\\max\\{u,0\\}\\),\n\\[\nf(x)=\n\\bigl(1-32\\operatorname{dist}(x,A)\\bigr)_+\n-\n\\bigl(1-32\\operatorname{dist}(Tx,A)\\bigr)_+,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nwhere each bump is zero at infinite distance.</p><p class=\"li\">At a tuple \\(\\mathbf q\\) in layer \\(i\\), define\n\\[\ng_{\\mathbf q}(x)\n=\n\\min_{y\\in\\Gamma^{M_i}}\n\\bigl(f(y)+L\\,d_\\infty(x,y)\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere \\(y\\) ranges over that tuple’s copy and \\(d_\\infty\\) is the sup circle metric. Clip \\(g_{\\mathbf q}\\) to \\([-1,1]\\), obtaining \\(\\widetilde g_{\\mathbf q}\\), and put\n\\[\nF_{\\mathbf q}(x)\n=\n\\frac{\\widetilde g_{\\mathbf q}(x)-\\widetilde g_{\\mathbf q}(Tx)}2.\n\\]\nFix the entire family \\((F_{\\mathbf q})\\) before sampling any occurrence chain; along a chain write \\(F_i=F_{\\mathbf q_i}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), and use the junta bound \\(J\\) of Lemma \\(2.1\\). Put\n\\[\nd=\\max\\{1,J(sL,\\gamma)(m+1)^s\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For every nonempty \\(I\\subseteq[r]\\) with \\(|I|\\le s\\), let \\(b_I=\\min I\\). For \\(t\\in\\mathcal T_m^I\\), define\n\\[\nh_{I,t}(\\theta)\n=\nF_{b_I}\\left(\n\\left(\n\\sum_{j\\in I}t_j\\theta_{j,\\pi_{b_Ij}(\\kappa)}\n\\pmod1\n\\right)_{\\kappa\\in M_{b_I}}\n\\right),\n\\quad\n\\theta\\in\\prod_{j\\in I}[0,1]^{M_j}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every occurrence chain, one can choose lists \\(S_I^j\\subseteq M_j\\), \\(j\\in I\\), with\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in I}|S_I^j|\\le d,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\ndepending only on the subchain’s label sets, internal projection maps, and functions \\(F_j\\), \\(j\\in I\\), such that every \\(h_{I,t}\\) has an \\(L^2\\)-approximating junta of error less than \\(\\gamma\\) using only scalar coordinates \\((j,\\ell)\\) with \\(\\ell\\in S_I^j\\). Identical subchain data receive identical choices.</p><p>Using the common label universe\n\\[\nM_r=\\Sigma_R^{r-1},\n\\]\ndefine terminal lists\n\\[\nA_I=\\bigcup_{j\\in I}\\pi_{jr}(S_I^j).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nThen \\(|A_I|\\le d\\), and for every fixed \\(I,J\\) with\n\\[\n1\\le|I|,|J|\\le s,\n\\qquad\n\\max I&lt;\\min J,\n\\]\na uniform occurrence chain satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{chain}}(A_I\\cap A_J\\ne\\varnothing)\\le d^2\\sigma.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(5.1\\) and \\(5.2\\) provide the functions and bounded lists. Equation (9) gives \\(|A_I|\\le d\\). It remains to prove the separated-list probability bound, which is the paper’s Lemma \\(4.2\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nh=\\max I&lt;\\min J\n\\]\nand condition on every occurrence except \\(c_h\\). The remaining occurrence is still uniform in the original instance.</p><p>At each layer in \\(I\\), position \\(h\\) of the tuple is the left question \\(u_h\\). Every internal projection between layers of \\(I\\) uses only tests with index less than \\(h\\). Thus all subchain data for \\(I\\), including its already fixed tuple functions, depend only on \\(u_h\\) and the conditioned background, not on the opposite question or on the projection of \\(c_h\\). Likewise, all data for \\(J\\) depend only on \\(v_h\\) and the background.</p><p>Form a list of candidate left answers by taking position \\(h\\) of every label in every \\(S_I^j\\), \\(j\\in I\\). This gives at most \\(d\\) elements of \\(\\Sigma_L\\), as a function of \\(u_h\\) and the fixed background. The analogous list for \\(J\\) gives at most \\(d\\) elements of \\(\\Sigma_R\\) as a function of \\(v_h\\). Choose deterministic orderings and pad each list to length \\(d\\) with an arbitrary answer, also when the list is empty.</p><p>If \\(A_I\\) and \\(A_J\\) intersect, some left and right selected labels have the same image at layer \\(r\\). Equality in position \\(h\\) says that \\(\\pi_{c_h}\\) sends the associated left candidate to the right candidate: on the left, exactly the test \\(c_h\\) acts in that position, and on the right the position is already a right-answer coordinate.</p><p>For each pair of list positions, selecting those answers for each own question is a deterministic strategy for the original Label Cover instance. Its success probability over uniform \\(c_h\\) is at most \\(\\sigma\\). A union bound over the \\(d^2\\) position pairs proves the conditional bound, and averaging over the background proves the proposition.</p><p>The fixed functions may depend on the entire instance and on \\(\\mathcal A\\). This causes no loss of locality: they are fixed before the sampling experiment, so evaluating the family at a tuple reveals only that tuple. In particular, if two occurrences have the same own question but different opposite questions or projections, the list rule at that side uses identical data.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The location sets satisfy \\( \\operatorname{dist}(A,TA)>1/8. \\) The function \\(f\\) takes values in \\([-1,1]\\), satisfies \\(f(Tx)=-f(x)\\), equals \\(1\\) on \\(A\\), is constant across every zero link, and is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the graph, independent set \\(\\mathcal A\\), location set \\(A\\), functions \\(f,F_{\\mathbf q}\\), and \\(L=64\\) from Proposition \\(5\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The location sets satisfy\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(A,TA)&gt;1/8.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe function \\(f\\) takes values in \\([-1,1]\\), satisfies \\(f(Tx)=-f(x)\\), equals \\(1\\) on \\(A\\), is constant across every zero link, and is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz within each grid copy for the sup circle metric.</p><p>Each \\(F_{\\mathbf q}\\) extends the corresponding grid data, is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz, takes values in \\([-1,1]\\), and is odd under \\(T\\). For every occurrence chain and every \\(i\\le j\\),\n\\[\n|F_j(x)-F_i(x\\circ\\pi_{ij})|\n\\le\\frac{2L}{D},\n\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb T^{M_j}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For locations of distinct selected vertices, (5) follows from the missing edge in (4). For a point compared with itself, it follows from exclusion of (2). Since the sets are finite, these pointwise strict inequalities give (5), including when distances are infinite.</p><p>The definition (6) takes values in \\([-1,1]\\), satisfies \\(f(Tx)=-f(x)\\), and equals \\(1\\) on \\(A\\). It is constant across every zero link.</p><p>Within each grid copy it is \\(64\\)-Lipschitz for the sup circle metric: distance to \\(A\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz on a component meeting \\(A\\), and its bump is identically zero on a component not meeting \\(A\\). Also the shortest-path pseudometric never exceeds a within-copy link distance.</p><p>By the real-valued Lipschitz extension formula of McShane [20], \\(g_{\\mathbf q}\\) is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz and equals \\(f\\) on the grid. Clipping preserves these properties. Antisymmetrization gives \\(F_{\\mathbf q}\\), which is still an extension of the odd grid data, is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz, is valued in \\([-1,1]\\), and is odd under \\(T\\).</p><p>To prove (7), round each coordinate of \\(x\\) once to a grid point \\(y\\) within \\(1/D\\). Pullback does not enlarge this error. The grid points \\(y\\) and \\(y\\circ\\pi_{ij}\\) have equal \\(f\\)-values by the chain of zero links, so the two extension errors total at most \\(2L/D\\). Intermediate layers contribute no additional approximation error.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are sets \\(S_I^j\\subseteq M_j\\), \\(j\\in I\\), satisfying \\( \\sum_{j\\in I}|S_I^j|\\le d, \\) such that, for every \\(t\\in\\mathcal T_m^I\\), a junta \\(g_{I,t}\\) using only scalar coordinates \\((j,\\ell)\\) with …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Lemma \\(4.1\\), “Bounded subchain lists,” of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Inherit the setting and functions \\(h_{I,t}\\) of Proposition \\(5\\), with \\(L=64\\), approximation tolerance \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\nd=\\max\\{1,J(sL,\\gamma)(m+1)^s\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an occurrence chain and a nonempty \\(I\\subseteq[r]\\) with \\(|I|\\le s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are sets \\(S_I^j\\subseteq M_j\\), \\(j\\in I\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in I}|S_I^j|\\le d,\n\\]\nsuch that, for every \\(t\\in\\mathcal T_m^I\\), a junta \\(g_{I,t}\\) using only scalar coordinates \\((j,\\ell)\\) with \\(\\ell\\in S_I^j\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|h_{I,t}-g_{I,t}\\|_2&lt;\\gamma.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nThe lists can be chosen as functions only of the subchain’s label sets, internal projection maps, and functions \\(F_j\\), \\(j\\in I\\). Identical subchain data receive identical choices.</p>", "proof": "<p>The entries of \\(\\theta\\) are independent uniform real variables. The phase map in (8) has Lipschitz constant at most \\(|I|\\le s\\): coordinate duplication by a projection cannot enlarge a sup distance. Thus \\(h_{I,t}\\) is \\(sL\\)-Lipschitz, independently of the label-set sizes and projection fibers.</p><p>For each of the at most \\((m+1)^s\\) coefficient choices, apply Lemma \\(2.1\\) to \\(h_{I,t}\\) with error \\(\\gamma\\). This uses at most \\(J(sL,\\gamma)\\) scalar coordinates. For each block \\(j\\), take the union of its selected coordinates over all coefficient choices to obtain \\(S_I^j\\) and the stated bound.</p><p>To make the choice depend only on the stated subchain data, order the scalar coordinates and choose the first subset of size at most \\(J(sL,\\gamma)\\) whose coordinate conditional expectation has error less than \\(\\gamma\\). Lemma \\(2.1\\) guarantees such a subset.</p><p>These choices enter only the soundness proof and are never computed by the reduction. In particular, a list includes approximating coordinates for every coefficient vector before any coefficients are sampled.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist an integer \\(r\\ge s\\) and a rational probability distribution \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\binom{[r]}s\\), chosen independently of the label universe and the list family, such that every list family satisfying (12) has …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Lemma \\(5.1\\), “Distributional alignment,” whose proof is given in Section \\(8\\) of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix integers \\(s,d\\ge1\\) and a real \\(\\eta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A list family on \\([r]\\) consists of sets \\(A_I\\subseteq M_*\\), \\(|A_I|\\le d\\), for every nonempty \\(I\\subseteq[r]\\) with \\(|I|\\le s\\), where \\(M_*\\) is any set.</p><p class=\"li\">The separated-disjointness condition is\n\\[\nA_I\\cap A_J=\\varnothing\n\\qquad\\text{whenever }\\max I&lt;\\min J.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(s\\)-element set \\(B\\subseteq[r]\\) is called bad if no map \\(a:M_*\\to B\\) satisfies\n\\[\na(A_I)\\subseteq I\n\\qquad\\text{for every nonempty }I\\subseteq B.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist an integer \\(r\\ge s\\) and a rational probability distribution \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\binom{[r]}s\\), chosen independently of the label universe and the list family, such that every list family satisfying (12) has bad-set probability at most \\(\\eta\\).</p><p>Equivalently, with probability at least \\(1-\\eta\\) over \\(B\\sim\\mu\\), a map satisfying (13) exists. The map may depend on the list family and on \\(B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For each label \\(x\\) appearing on a list indexed inside \\(B\\), consider\n\\[\n\\bigcap\\{I:\\varnothing\\ne I\\subseteq B,\\ x\\in A_I\\}.\n\\]\nThe set \\(B\\) is bad exactly when one of these intersections is empty. Indeed, a nonempty intersection permits a choice of \\(a(x)\\) independently for each appearing label; labels absent from all these lists may be sent to any element of the nonempty set \\(B\\).</p><p>First prove that, for every \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), some \\(r\\ge s\\) admits a real probability distribution under which every list family satisfying (12) has bad-set probability at most \\(\\xi\\).</p><p>If this were false, Lemma \\(6.1\\) would give random allowed patterns satisfying (32) for every \\(r\\). Lemma \\(6.2\\) would then produce an order-invariant random pattern on the rationals in which every fixed rational \\(s\\)-set has bad probability at least \\(\\xi\\). Lemma \\(6.3\\) shows that no such set is bad, contradicting \\(\\xi&gt;0\\). This proves the assertion with real probabilities.</p><p>Choose \\(0&lt;\\xi&lt;\\eta\\) and obtain \\(r\\) and a real distribution \\(\\mu_0\\) with bad-set probability at most \\(\\xi\\) for every allowed pattern. Approximate \\(\\mu_0\\) by a rational point \\(\\mu\\) of the same finite simplex with\n\\[\n\\sum_{B\\in\\binom{[r]}s}\n|\\mu(B)-\\mu_0(B)|&lt;\\eta-\\xi.\n\\]\nEvery bad-set indicator takes values in \\([0,1]\\), so this changes its expectation by less than \\(\\eta-\\xi\\), uniformly over all patterns. Thus \\(\\mu\\) has the required guarantee.</p><p>As recorded in Remark \\(8.1\\) of the paper, for rational \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) the finite data can also be found by a terminating search: enumerate \\(r\\ge s\\), enumerate its finitely many allowed slot patterns, and minimize their maximum bad-set probability by a rational linear program. Search until the optimum is less than \\(\\eta\\). The proof with a smaller tolerance guarantees termination. No bound on the label universe, or oracle describing it, is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(r\\ge s\\), there is a random allowed pattern \\(C_r\\) such that \\( \\Pr(B\\text{ is bad in }C_r)\\ge\\xi \\qquad \\text{for every }B\\in\\binom{[r]}s. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit \\(s,d\\) and separated disjointness from Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On a finite ordered index set, order each list arbitrarily and retain only its length and the equality relation among occupied slots. Each list has at most \\(d\\) slots, and occupied slots within a list are distinct. Such data are called a pattern.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathscr C_r\\) be the finite set of patterns on \\([r]\\) satisfying separated disjointness.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(B\\in\\binom{[r]}s\\) and \\(C\\in\\mathscr C_r\\), let\n\\[\nb(B,C)=\\mathbf1_{\\{B\\text{ is bad in }C\\}}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\xi&gt;0\\). Assume that, for every \\(r\\ge s\\), no real probability distribution on \\(\\binom{[r]}s\\) makes the bad-set probability at most \\(\\xi\\) for every allowed list family.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(r\\ge s\\), there is a random allowed pattern \\(C_r\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Pr(B\\text{ is bad in }C_r)\\ge\\xi\n\\qquad\n\\text{for every }B\\in\\binom{[r]}s.\n\\tag{32}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every actual list family gives a pattern, and conversely a pattern is realized by using its equivalence classes as labels. Thus the original label universe has no further role in either separation or badness.</p><p>Under the assumed failure, finite minimax [26] gives, for each \\(r\\ge s\\),\n\\[\n\\min_\\nu\\max_{C\\in\\mathscr C_r}\n\\mathbb E_{B\\sim\\nu}b(B,C)\n=\n\\max_\\rho\\min_{B\\in\\binom{[r]}s}\n\\mathbb E_{C\\sim\\rho}b(B,C)\n&gt;\n\\xi.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\nu\\) and \\(\\rho\\) range over the two finite probability simplices. Their compactness ensures that the extrema are attained. A maximizing \\(\\rho\\) gives a random allowed pattern satisfying (32).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a countable random array of list lengths and equality relations, indexed by the nonempty \\(I\\subseteq\\mathbb Q\\) with \\(|I|\\le s\\), such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(s,d\\ge1\\) and \\(\\xi&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that, for every \\(r\\ge s\\), there is a random pattern \\(C_r\\) satisfying separated disjointness and (32), as in Lemma \\(6.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t\\ge1\\), let \\(\\mathscr P_t\\) be the finite set of all allowed patterns on \\([t]\\), using lists on nonempty subsets of size at most \\(s\\). Restrictions are followed by increasing identification of their index sets with initial intervals of integers.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a countable random array of list lengths and equality relations, indexed by the nonempty \\(I\\subseteq\\mathbb Q\\) with \\(|I|\\le s\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li\">all equivalence-relation axioms, distinctness within a list, and separated disjointness hold simultaneously almost surely;</p><p class=\"li\">its law is invariant under every increasing automorphism of \\(\\mathbb Q\\);</p><p class=\"li\">every fixed rational \\(s\\)-element set has bad probability at least \\(\\xi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(k\\ge s\\). For each \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) and each \\(t\\)-subset \\(J\\) of \\([r]\\), the restriction of \\(C_r\\) to \\(J\\) has a probability vector in the finite simplex on \\(\\mathscr P_t\\). Color \\(J\\) by this vector with every entry placed in a bin of width at most \\(1/k\\). The number of colors depends only on \\(s,d,t,k\\), not on \\(r\\).</p><p>Successive applications of the finite Ramsey theorem [23], with the required intermediate set sizes chosen backwards, give the following when \\(r\\) is sufficiently large: there is a \\(k\\)-element set \\(H_k\\) on which all the colorings, for \\(1\\le t\\le k\\), are homogeneous. Passing to a subset preserves each homogeneity already obtained. In particular, the induced pattern laws on any two \\(t\\)-subsets of \\(H_k\\) differ by at most \\(1/k\\) in every coordinate.</p><p>Let \\(P_t^{(k)}\\) be the law on the first \\(t\\) points of \\(H_k\\). If \\(t\\le u\\le k\\) and \\(J\\in\\binom{[u]}t\\), restricting \\(P_u^{(k)}\\) to \\(J\\) gives the law on the corresponding \\(t\\)-subset of \\(H_k\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\bigl(P_u^{(k)}|_J\\bigr)(C)-P_t^{(k)}(C)\n\\right|\n\\le\\frac1k,\n\\qquad C\\in\\mathscr P_t.\n\\tag{33}\n\\]</p><p>Compactness of each finite simplex and a diagonal subsequence as \\(k\\to\\infty\\) produce limiting laws \\(P_t\\) for every \\(t\\). Taking limits in (33) gives exact consistency under every ordered restriction:\n\\[\nP_u|_J=P_t\n\\qquad\n\\left(t\\le u,\\ J\\in\\binom{[u]}t\\right).\n\\tag{34}\n\\]\nMoreover, (32) gives\n\\[\nP_s(\\text{the full index set is bad})\\ge\\xi.\n\\tag{35}\n\\]</p><p>Assign the law \\(P_t\\) to every increasing \\(t\\)-tuple of rational indices. The consistency in (34) constructs a countable random array of list lengths and equality relations indexed by nonempty \\(I\\subseteq\\mathbb Q\\) of size at most \\(s\\).</p><p>For completeness, enumerate \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and successively extend the pattern on the first \\(n\\) enumerated indices to the first \\(n+1\\), using the conditional probabilities supplied by their consistent finite laws. Choices on zero-probability conditioning events are immaterial. Every finite set of rational indices then has its specified law.</p><p>All equivalence-relation axioms, distinctness within a list, and separated disjointness hold simultaneously almost surely: each violation involves finitely many indices, and there are only countably many possible violations. The array’s law is invariant under every increasing automorphism of \\(\\mathbb Q\\), because its finite laws depend only on order. Equation (35) says that every fixed rational \\(s\\)-set still has bad probability at least \\(\\xi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Almost surely, every equivalence class of occupied slots has an index common to all sets on whose lists it occurs.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a countable random array of lists and equality relations on nonempty \\(I\\subseteq\\mathbb Q\\), \\(|I|\\le s\\), have at most \\(d\\) slots in each list.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that equivalence-relation axioms, distinctness within lists, and separated disjointness hold simultaneously almost surely.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that its law is invariant under every increasing automorphism of \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Almost surely, every equivalence class of occupied slots has an index common to all sets on whose lists it occurs. Consequently, no finite rational \\(s\\)-element set is bad.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a nonempty \\(I\\subseteq\\mathbb Q\\), \\(|I|\\le s\\), and one of its \\(d\\) possible slots. Let \\(E\\) be the event that this slot is occupied. On \\(E\\), write \\(\\mathcal L\\) for its equivalence class of occupied slots, and define\n\\[\np=\\sup\\{\\min J:\\text{a slot at }J\\text{ belongs to }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\tag{36}\n\\]\nThe set whose supremum is taken contains \\(\\min I\\). It is bounded above by \\(\\max I\\), since an occurrence at \\(J\\) with \\(\\min J&gt;\\max I\\) would violate separated disjointness. Thus\n\\[\n\\min I\\le p\\le\\max I\n\\qquad\\text{on }E.\n\\tag{37}\n\\]</p><p>The random variable \\(p\\) is measurable on \\(E\\): the occurrence family is countable, and testing whether its supremum is at most a given real number is a countable intersection of measurable conditions on slots.</p><p>Every increasing automorphism \\(g\\) of \\(\\mathbb Q\\) extends uniquely to an increasing homeomorphism \\(\\bar g\\) of \\(\\mathbb R\\). Transporting the array by \\(g\\) transports the occurrence family in (36) and sends its supremum to \\(\\bar g(p)\\). If \\(g\\) fixes \\(I\\) pointwise, it also fixes the selected slot and its occupancy event. Hence the finite measure\n\\[\n\\nu(U)=\\Pr(E\\text{ and }p\\in U),\n\\qquad U\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\text{ Borel},\n\\]\nis invariant under all such \\(\\bar g\\). This formulation also covers \\(\\Pr(E)=0\\) without conditioning on a null event.</p><p>Let \\(a&lt;b\\) be consecutive points of \\(I\\). For any rational \\(a&lt;q&lt;q'&lt;b\\), there is an increasing automorphism of \\(\\mathbb Q\\) fixing \\(I\\) pointwise and carrying \\(q\\) to \\(q'\\). For example, use a piecewise linear increasing bijection of \\(\\mathbb R\\) that is the identity off \\([a,b]\\), maps \\(q\\) to \\(q'\\), and is linear on \\([a,q]\\) and \\([q,b]\\). Its rational breakpoints and rational slopes make it a bijection of \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p>Invariance gives\n\\[\n\\nu((-\\infty,q])=\\nu((-\\infty,q']),\n\\qquad\n\\nu((q,q'])=0.\n\\]\nCountably many such rational intervals cover \\((a,b)\\), whence \\(\\nu((a,b))=0\\). Together with (37), this proves\n\\[\n\\Pr(E\\text{ and }p\\notin I)=0.\n\\tag{38}\n\\]\nIf \\(I\\) is a singleton, (38) follows directly from (37).</p><p>There are only countably many possible slots, so (38) holds simultaneously at every occupied slot with probability one. Equivalent slots have exactly the same occurrence family and hence the same supremum \\(p\\). It follows that every equivalence class has a common index in all the sets on whose lists it occurs.</p><p>In particular, on every finite rational \\(s\\)-set \\(B\\), every label appearing inside \\(B\\) has a nonempty intersection of its occurrence sets there. Thus no such \\(B\\) is bad.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The probability that the pair is not aligned is at most \\( 4^r d^2\\sigma+\\varepsilon. \\) For a fixed aligned pair, the alignment can be chosen before drawing coefficients or rotations.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Lemma \\(5.3\\), “Aligned pairs occur with high probability,” using Definition \\(5.2\\), of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a Label Cover instance with value at most \\(\\sigma\\in(0,1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s,d\\ge1\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). Choose \\(r\\ge s\\) and a rational law \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\binom{[r]}s\\) from Proposition \\(6\\), with \\(\\eta=\\varepsilon\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((A_I)\\) be the terminal lists extracted by Proposition \\(5\\) from a fixed independent set, with \\(|A_I|\\le d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sample a uniform occurrence chain and, independently, \\(B\\sim\\mu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A chain–\\(B\\) pair is aligned if there is a map\n\\[\na:M_r\\longrightarrow B\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\na(A_I)\\subseteq I\n\\qquad\n\\text{for every nonempty }I\\subseteq B.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The probability that the pair is not aligned is at most\n\\[\n4^r d^2\\sigma+\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nFor a fixed aligned pair, the alignment can be chosen before drawing coefficients or rotations.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are at most \\(4^r\\) ordered pairs of subsets of \\([r]\\). By Proposition \\(5\\) and a union bound, the probability that some separated pair of terminal lists intersects is at most\n\\[\n4^r d^2\\sigma.\n\\]\nFor each chain with no such intersection, Proposition \\(6\\) gives an alignment except for a set of \\(B\\) of \\(\\mu\\)-probability at most \\(\\varepsilon\\). Adding the two failure probabilities proves the bound.</p><p>The lists were selected before \\(B\\), and include the juntas for every coefficient choice. An alignment for a fixed aligned pair can therefore be chosen before the actual coefficient and rotation draws.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditional on this chain and \\(B\\), the probability that the sampled output vertex belongs to \\(\\mathcal A\\) is at most \\(6\\varepsilon\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Proposition \\(6.1\\) of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the graph construction of Proposition \\(3\\), with \\(r\\ge s\\ge2\\), coefficient grid \\(\\mathcal T_m\\), probabilities \\(p_k\\), rational \\(\\lambda\\in(0,1)\\), even \\(P\\), and \\(D=mP\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that the construction does not take the clique branch. Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be a nonempty independent set of the output graph.</p><p class=\"li\">Form the functions \\(f,F_i\\), subchain functions \\(h_{I,t}\\), lists \\(S_I^j\\), juntas \\(g_{I,t}\\), and terminal lists \\(A_I\\) associated with \\(\\mathcal A\\) as in Proposition \\(5\\). Thus \\(L=64\\), every \\(F_i\\) is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz and odd, and\n\\[\n|F_j(x)-F_i(x\\circ\\pi_{ij})|\\le2L/D.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and take \\(\\beta&gt;0\\), \\(K\\ge1\\) from Proposition \\(2\\) for \\(L,\\varepsilon\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) be the list-approximation tolerance. Assume\n\\[\n\\frac Lm&lt;\\frac\\beta2,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda K&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n(1-p_m)^s&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{s(2\\gamma)^2}{\\beta^2}&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{2L}{D}&lt;\\gamma,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{2Ls}{P}&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an occurrence chain and an \\(s\\)-element set \\(B\\) admitting a map\n\\[\na:M_r\\longrightarrow B,\n\\qquad\na(A_I)\\subseteq I\n\\quad\n\\text{for every nonempty }I\\subseteq B.\n\\]\nFix this map before drawing coefficients or rotations, and put \\(b=\\min B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Conditional on this chain and \\(B\\), the probability that the sampled output vertex belongs to \\(\\mathcal A\\) is at most \\(6\\varepsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(f=1\\) at every location of \\(\\mathcal A\\), it suffices to bound the expected square of \\(F_b\\) at the sampled grid location by \\(6\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Use the continuous rotation experiment and auxiliary phases of Lemma \\(8.1\\). The parameter choices give\n\\[\n\\Pr(t\\notin\\mathcal F)=(1-p_m)^s&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nBy (17), the event \\(X(t,\\theta)&gt;\\varepsilon\\) is contained in the union of this event and the two events bounded in Lemmas \\(8.2\\) and \\(8.3\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\Pr_{t,\\theta}(X(t,\\theta)&gt;\\varepsilon)&lt;3\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\le X\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{t,\\theta,z}H(t,\\theta;z)^2\n=\n\\mathbb E_{t,\\theta}X(t,\\theta)\n\\le\n\\varepsilon+\\Pr(X&gt;\\varepsilon)\n&lt;\n4\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{26}\n\\]</p><p>Define the continuous location\n\\[\nx(t,\\theta)\n=\n\\left(\n\\sum_{j\\in B}t_j\\theta_{j,\\pi_{bj}(\\kappa)}\n\\pmod1\n\\right)_{\\kappa\\in M_b}.\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(t\\in\\mathcal F\\), choose \\(j\\) with \\(t_j=1\\). Applying the translation (19) to this block absorbs every \\(z\\)-term in (15). For each fixed \\(z\\), the translated rotation array again has exactly its original product law. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\theta,z}H(t,\\theta;z)^2\n=\n\\mathbb E_\\theta F_b(x(t,\\theta))^2\n\\qquad(t\\in\\mathcal F).\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\nThe choice of full block may depend on \\(t\\), because this equality is asserted separately for every fixed coefficient vector. In particular, (26) implies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{t,\\theta}\n\\bigl[\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal F}(t)F_b(x(t,\\theta))^2\\bigr]\n&lt;\n4\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{28}\n\\]</p><p>Couple the discrete rotations to the continuous ones by\n\\[\n\\widehat\\theta_{j,\\ell}\n=\n\\frac{\\lfloor P\\theta_{j,\\ell}\\rfloor}{P}.\n\\]\nThey have the required independent uniform grid law. Each input coordinate changes by less than \\(1/P\\), so the sup circle distance between \\(x(t,\\theta)\\) and \\(x(t,\\widehat\\theta)\\) is at most \\(s/P\\). The Lipschitz bound and range \\([-1,1]\\) imply\n\\[\n\\left|\nF_b(x(t,\\theta))^2\n-\nF_b(x(t,\\widehat\\theta))^2\n\\right|\n\\le\n2Ls/P\n&lt;\n\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\nCombining (28), (29), and \\(\\Pr(t\\notin\\mathcal F)&lt;\\varepsilon\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{t,\\widehat\\theta}\nF_b(x(t,\\widehat\\theta))^2\n&lt;\n4\\varepsilon+\\varepsilon+\\varepsilon\n=\n6\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{30}\n\\]</p><p>The discrete location belongs to the grid of denominator \\(D=mP\\), so \\(F_b\\) equals the original grid function \\(f\\) there. That function equals one at every location of a vertex of \\(\\mathcal A\\). Thus the indicator that the sampled vertex belongs to \\(\\mathcal A\\) is pointwise bounded by\n\\[\nF_b(x(t,\\widehat\\theta))^2.\n\\]\nThis remains true when several distinct vertices have the same location. Inequality (30) proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(t,\\theta\\), the function \\(H(t,\\theta;\\cdot)\\) is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz, takes values in \\([-1,1]\\), and satisfies \\( \\mathbb E_zH(t,\\theta;z)=0. \\) Consequently …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the fixed chain, set \\(B\\), map \\(a\\), functions \\(F_i\\), and constants of Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sample \\(t\\in\\mathcal T_m^B\\) with independent coordinates and law (1).</p><p class=\"li\">Replace the discrete rotations by independent uniform real variables\n\\[\n\\theta_{j,\\ell}\\in[0,1),\n\\qquad j\\in B,\\ \\ell\\in M_j,\n\\]\nindependent of \\(t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Introduce independent Haar-uniform variables \\(z=(z_i)_{i\\in B}\\in\\mathbb T^B\\), independent of \\(t,\\theta\\), and define\n\\[\nH(t,\\theta;z)\n=\nF_b\\left(\n\\left(\nz_{a(\\pi_{br}(\\kappa))}\n+\n\\sum_{j\\in B}t_j\\theta_{j,\\pi_{bj}(\\kappa)}\n\\pmod1\n\\right)_{\\kappa\\in M_b}\n\\right).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_i\\) average only \\(z_i\\), let \\(Q_i=\\operatorname{id}-E_i\\), and set\n\\[\nD_i(t,\\theta)=\\|Q_iH(t,\\theta;\\cdot)\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb T^B)},\n\\]\n\\[\nX(t,\\theta)=\\mathbb E_zH(t,\\theta;z)^2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(t,\\theta\\), the function \\(H(t,\\theta;\\cdot)\\) is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz, takes values in \\([-1,1]\\), and satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_zH(t,\\theta;z)=0.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nX(t,\\theta)&gt;\\varepsilon\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\max_{i\\in B}D_i(t,\\theta)\\ge\\beta,\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\#\\{i\\in B:D_i(t,\\theta)\\ge\\beta/2\\}\\le K\n\\quad\\text{for every }t,\\theta.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(t,\\theta\\), the map from \\(z\\) to the phase vector in (15) is nonexpanding for the sup circle metrics: each output coordinate uses just one coordinate of \\(z\\). Consequently \\(H\\) is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz as a function of \\(z\\), independently of \\(s\\) and of all label cardinalities. It is bounded by one in absolute value.</p><p>A simultaneous half-shift of the \\(z\\)-coordinates half-shifts every input coordinate of \\(F_b\\), so oddness gives (16). Applying Proposition \\(2\\) gives (17) and (18).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and \\(i\\in B\\) with \\(t_i=0\\), \\( \\mathbb E_\\theta D_i(t,\\theta)^2<(2\\gamma)^2. \\) Therefore …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the setting and the variables \\(t,\\theta,z,H,D_i\\) of Proposition \\(8\\) and Lemma \\(8.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{t:\\text{some }t_j=1\\}.\n\\]\nA coefficient equal to \\(1\\) is called full.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and \\(i\\in B\\) with \\(t_i=0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\theta D_i(t,\\theta)^2&lt;(2\\gamma)^2.\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\Pr_{t,\\theta}\n\\bigl(t\\in\\mathcal F,\\ \\exists i\\in B:\nt_i=0,\\ D_i(t,\\theta)\\ge\\beta\\bigr)\n\\le\n\\frac{s(2\\gamma)^2}{\\beta^2}\n&lt;\n\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and \\(i\\in B\\) with \\(t_i=0\\). Choose \\(j\\in B\\) with \\(t_j=1\\); necessarily \\(j\\ne i\\). Put\n\\[\nI=B\\setminus\\{i\\},\n\\qquad\nc=\\min I.\n\\]\nThe set \\(I\\) is nonempty.</p><p>In the subchain function \\(h_{I,t|_I}\\), leave all rotation blocks unchanged except block \\(j\\), where substitute\n\\[\n\\theta'_{j,\\ell}\n=\n\\theta_{j,\\ell}+z_{a(\\pi_{jr}(\\ell))}\n\\pmod1,\n\\qquad \\ell\\in M_j.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\nA phase used as a real rotation is represented in \\([0,1)\\). For each fixed \\(z\\), this substitution preserves the product uniform distribution of the rotation inputs. Even when several coordinates receive the same shift, each coordinate undergoes a fixed translation, so their conditional joint law remains the same product law.</p><p>Let \\(y\\in\\mathbb T^{M_c}\\) be the phase vector in \\(h_{I,t|_I}(\\theta')\\). For every \\(\\kappa\\in M_b\\), composition of the projections gives\n\\[\ny_{\\pi_{bc}(\\kappa)}\n=\nz_{a(\\pi_{br}(\\kappa))}\n+\n\\sum_{k\\in B}t_k\\theta_{k,\\pi_{bk}(\\kappa)}\n\\pmod1.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\nIndeed, the omitted \\(i\\)-term is zero, and the changed \\(j\\)-term has coefficient exactly one. This last fact is essential: reducing the shifted rotation modulo one before multiplying would not in general be valid at a fractional coefficient.</p><p>Thus\n\\[\nH(t,\\theta;z)=F_b(y\\circ\\pi_{bc}).\n\\]\nIf \\(c=b\\), the two evaluations are identical; if deleting \\(i\\) changes the minimum layer, compatibility (7) gives\n\\[\n|H(t,\\theta;z)-h_{I,t|_I}(\\theta')|\n\\le\n2L/D\n&lt;\n\\gamma.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(g_{I,t|_I}\\) be the fixed junta approximating \\(h_{I,t|_I}\\) in \\(L^2\\)-norm to error less than \\(\\gamma\\). Its selected coordinates in block \\(k\\) belong to \\(S_I^k\\). Product-law preservation for every fixed \\(z\\) implies\n\\[\n\\|h_{I,t|_I}(\\theta')-g_{I,t|_I}(\\theta')\\|_{L^2(\\theta,z)}\n&lt;\n\\gamma.\n\\]</p><p>Moreover, \\(g_{I,t|_I}(\\theta')\\) is pointwise independent of \\(z_i\\). Only the modified block \\(j\\) introduces any \\(z\\)-dependence. Every selected coordinate \\(\\ell\\in S_I^j\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\pi_{jr}(\\ell)\\in A_I,\n\\]\nand alignment therefore gives\n\\[\na(\\pi_{jr}(\\ell))\\in I.\n\\]</p><p>Combining this observation with (21) and applying the contraction \\(Q_i\\) on the full product space yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\theta D_i(t,\\theta)^2\n=\n\\|Q_iH\\|_{L^2(\\theta,z)}^2\n\\le\n\\|H-g_{I,t|_I}(\\theta')\\|_{L^2(\\theta,z)}^2\n&lt;\n(2\\gamma)^2.\n\\]\nThis holds for every fixed \\(t\\in\\mathcal F\\) with \\(t_i=0\\). Markov’s inequality followed by a union bound over the \\(s\\) indices proves (23).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\Pr_{t,\\theta} \\bigl(\\exists i\\in B:t_i>0,\\ D_i(t,\\theta)\\ge\\beta\\bigr) \\le \\lambda K < \\varepsilon. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the setting and variables \\(t,\\theta,H,D_i\\) of Proposition \\(8\\) and Lemma \\(8.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The coefficient probabilities satisfy \\(p_k=\\lambda p_{k-1}\\) for \\(1\\le k\\le m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">No assumption that a full coefficient is present is added.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr_{t,\\theta}\n\\bigl(\\exists i\\in B:t_i&gt;0,\\ D_i(t,\\theta)\\ge\\beta\\bigr)\n\\le\n\\lambda K\n&lt;\n\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{25}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(i\\), all rotations \\(\\theta\\), and all coefficients other than \\(t_i\\). For \\(0\\le k\\le m\\), let \\(H_k\\) denote (15) with \\(t_i=k/m\\), and put\n\\[\nd_k=\\|Q_iH_k\\|_2.\n\\]\nChanging one coefficient by \\(1/m\\) changes each phase coordinate by circle distance at most \\(1/m\\), since the rotations lie in \\([0,1)\\). The norm-one property of \\(Q_i\\) gives\n\\[\n|d_k-d_{k-1}|\n\\le\n\\|Q_i(H_k-H_{k-1})\\|_2\n\\le\nL/m\n&lt;\n\\beta/2\n\\qquad(1\\le k\\le m).\n\\tag{24}\n\\]</p><p>Since \\(p_k=\\lambda p_{k-1}\\), (24) implies, for the fixed background data,\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^m p_k\\mathbf1_{\\{d_k\\ge\\beta\\}}\n\\le\n\\lambda\\sum_{h=0}^{m-1}p_h\\mathbf1_{\\{d_h\\ge\\beta/2\\}}\n\\le\n\\lambda\\sum_{h=0}^m p_h\\mathbf1_{\\{d_h\\ge\\beta/2\\}}.\n\\]\nIntegrating over the fixed data and summing over \\(i\\in B\\), equation (18) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\Pr_{t,\\theta}\n\\bigl(\\exists i\\in B:t_i&gt;0,\\ D_i(t,\\theta)\\ge\\beta\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\le\n\\lambda\\sum_{i\\in B}\n\\Pr_{t,\\theta}(D_i(t,\\theta)\\ge\\beta/2)\n\\le\n\\lambda K\n&lt;\n\\varepsilon.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>Decreasing a level can destroy the only full coefficient. This causes no restriction here: (18) holds for every coefficient vector, including all images of the one-level decrease.</p>"}], "edges": [[4, 3, "part"], [8, 7, "part"], [9, 7, "part"], [11, 10, "part"], [12, 10, "part"], [13, 10, "part"], [16, 15, "part"], [17, 15, "part"], [18, 15, "part"]]});
